  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bridge Phase &#x2161; Programme (Timor-Leste) : Enhancing Quality of Non-formal Education and Harness ICT for Youth in Timor-Leste</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to enhance access to basic education for out-of-school youth and marginalised communities in Timor-Leste. By supporting non-formal education, it seeks to improve literacy, vocational skills, and digital literacy, contributing to SDG 4 (Quality Education).  In collaboration with UNESCO National Commissions, the project will strengthen Timor-Lestes non-formal education policies and establish community learning centers (CLCs) across the country. Timor-Leste, classified as a least developed country, faces high youth unemployment, infrastructure gaps, and educational disparities. Many young people, especially in rural areas, lack access to formal education, while literacy rates remain low, particularly among disadvantaged groups.  Although the government has prioritised literacy reduction and alternative education pathways, limited resources and teaching capacity hinder progress. Additionally, the high demand for Korean language education, driven by the Korea-Timor-Leste Employment Permit System, highlights the need for diversified learning opportunities.  This project addresses these gaps by developing policies for secondary education accreditation and teacher training, providing ICT-based learning through computers and tablets, and offering Korean language classes. By expanding learning opportunities and building local capacity through teacher training and stakeholder workshops, the initiative aims to bridge educational gaps, reduce poverty, and create long-term improvements for Timor-Lestes underserved communities.</narrative>
  </description>
